import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const methodCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Question → hypothesis', note: 'A hypothesis must be falsifiable — state what result would prove it wrong (Karl Popper\'s demand, 1934). "Unfalsifiable" means unscientific.' },
    { math: '2 · Operationalise the variables', note: 'Turn ideas into measurable things: "stress" becomes cortisol level or a validated questionnaire. Vague measures produce vague science.' },
    { math: '3 · Control group + random assignment', note: 'The comparison group receives no treatment; randomness distributes every other difference — motivation, sleep, luck — evenly between groups.' },
    { math: '4 · Blinding', note: 'Participants and, where possible, experimenters must not know who got the treatment. Expectation alone can produce real measured effects (the placebo problem).' },
    { math: '5 · Pre-register, then replicate', note: 'Announce the analysis before seeing data; then run it again on new people. A finding that only one lab can produce is a rumour.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/research-methods">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Psychology's hardest problem is that its subject — a person — knows it's being studied, has
    moods, and differs from every other person. The discipline's answer is method: a set of
    procedures for telling real effects from expectations, coincidences and wishful thinking.
    The five steps here are the skeleton of every honest experiment.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five-step skeleton of an experiment" widget="equationStep" config={methodCfg} />

  <h2>Correlation is not causation — the full argument</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reverse direction:</strong> does anxiety cause insomnia, or insomnia anxiety?</li>
    <li><strong>Third variables:</strong> ice-cream drownings rise together because of summer, not dessert.</li>
    <li><strong>Selection:</strong> hospital patients are sicker than the general public — because that is why they are there.</li>
    <li>Only <strong>randomised experiments</strong> break these traps, which is exactly why step 3 exists.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How psychology polices itself</h2>
  <p>
    The field has faced its methods honestly: the <strong>replication crisis</strong> surfaced in
    2011–2015 when a 100-study crowdsourced rerun (Open Science Collaboration, 2015) could
    reproduce only about a third of famous findings. The response reshaped practice — larger
    samples, pre-registration, open data. Related caution: most participants have historically
    come from <strong>WEIRD</strong> populations (Western, educated, industrialised, rich,
    democratic — Henrich et al., 2010), and findings don't always travel. Good psychology now
    treats both as first-order problems.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Popper, The Logic of Scientific Discovery; Open Science Collaboration, Science (2015); Henrich, Heine & Norenzayan, BBS (2010).</p>
</TopicPage>
